WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.160
1979 was a momentous year in the Middle East — the Egyptian-Israeli peace treaty,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.540
Saddam Hussein becoming president of Iraq, and the Soviet invasion of Afghanistan.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:19.289
But in retrospect, the most significant event was the Iranian revolution, an anniversary

00:00:19.289 --> 00:00:20.870
we mark on February 11.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:25.259
The revolution removed the Shah, who
had been our chief proxy in this strategically

00:00:25.259 --> 00:00:28.240
vital but highly volatile part of the world.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.550
Worse, it brought to power a regime that has
defined the US as its principal

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:37.620
enemy ever since, despite repeated efforts
by American administrations to find a path

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:38.999
toward peace.

00:00:38.999 --> 00:00:44.289
Since 1979, the most constant threat we've faced in the region has been Iran’s relentless

00:00:44.289 --> 00:00:48.120
enmity — which it has aggressively pursued
by killing Americans,

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:53.129
attacking American military forces and allies,
and arming groups that seek to overturn the

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:55.079
regional status quo by violence.

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:58.949
As Americans debate our role in the
Middle East, it’s worth remembering why

00:00:58.949 --> 00:01:01.229
many fear it would be dangerous to leave.

00:01:01.229 --> 00:01:06.159
Iran’s dogged aggression against the United
States, its friends and interests, which started

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.820
40 years ago.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.920
What do you think the future holds for US-Iran relations?

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:13.580
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:18.180
Also, let us know what other topics you’d
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.300
and be sure to like and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

